DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/faculty-candidate-talk-sentin
 el-and-storyteller-black-womens-cinematic-memory-work-yasmina-price-presid
 ential-postdoctoral-research-fellow-princeton-university\n\nAt the interst
 ices of transnational Black feminism and Third Cinema\, filmmakers Sarah M
 aldoror and Sara Gómez authored an alternative story of liberation. Both 
 contributors to the cultural front of anticolonial militancy and class str
 uggle in the 1960s-70s\, they recast prevailing nationalist and masculinis
 t norms. Their cinematic memory work of witnessing and retelling illuminat
 es a meaningful Black feminist genealogy within African and Afro-diasporic
  cinemas. Through hybrid and polyvocal short documentaries\, Maldoror and 
 Gómez produced a kinetic archive\, which demands a critical\, pluralistic
  recovery through the interdependence of scholarship\, public programming 
 and material restoration. Revisiting Maldoror and Gómez situates Black wo
 men’s aesthetic\, political and theoretical engagements with history and
  visuality carried forward by contemporary filmmakers and visual artists.\
 n\nYasmina Price is a writer\, film programmer and scholar of African & Af
 ro-diasporic visual culture. Her work pays particular attention to the mat
 eriality of image-making\, aesthetic historiography and the experimental p
 ractices of women filmmakers and artists. Her writing has appeared in Film
  Quarterly\, World Records Journal\, The Nation\, frieze\, Criterion’s C
 urrent and elsewhere. Her programming and curatorial work includes Antholo
 gy Film Archives\, the Brooklyn Academy of Music\, the Schomburg Center fo
 r Research in Black Culture\, the Los Angeles County Museum of Art and the
  Pan African Film & Arts Festiv
